contentText:
To the very end of her life, Dolly Parton was determined to be a source of light in the world, even if it meant eschewing a traditional funeral and sharing details about her health struggles with her fans, or even her family. Parton&#8217;s first cousin, occasional musical collaborator and the family&#8217;s archivist, Richie Owens, told the Today Show on Thursday (Sept. 3) that the beloved country icon who died on Aug. 25 at age 80 after a brief battle with cancer kept even her kin in the dark about her failing health, believing that projecting an upbeat, positive mood for everyone around was the best option.

